<Base title="The name"
      eyebrow="2,124 in the world"
      dek="Lerena is a rare surname — rare enough that rarity itself becomes a research tool. It may also be two separate names sharing one spelling. Both facts shape everything else in this archive.">
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-how-rare">How rare</h2>
  <p>
    Forebears, accessed 8 September 2026: <strong>2,124 bearers worldwide</strong>, the
    <strong>197,177th</strong> commonest surname on earth, an incidence of <strong>1 in 3,431,048</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set that beside the other families in this group of archives. <em>Falco</em> has
    <strong>46,749</strong> bearers and two independent origins, so no two Falco lines can be assumed
    related. <em>Lerena</em> is <strong>twenty-two times scarcer</strong>. The methodological consequence
    runs the other way:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    With a surname this rare, a cluster of Lerenas in one town is far more likely to be one family than to
    be coincidence. Connections may be <em>proposed</em> on distribution alone — but they must still be
    <em>proved</em> in the registers.
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="contents">What is on this page</h2>
  <p class="dek">Where the surname is, where it is not, and what that is worth as evidence. The build checks this list against the page, so a section cannot be added or
    renamed without appearing here.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <ol class="cols2">
      <li><a href="#s-how-rare">How rare</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-where-they-are">Where they are</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-spain-one-cluster-and-it-is-not-where-you-would-guess">Spain: one cluster, and it is not where you would guess</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-what-the-name-means-the-unresolved-fork">What the name means — the unresolved fork</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-who-the-lerenas-married-at-trinidad">Who the Lerenas married at Trinidad</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-sources-for-this-page">Sources for this page</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-two-spellings-one-town">Two spellings, one town</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s-the-forename-is-the-sharper-clue">The forename is the sharper clue</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-where-they-are">Where they are</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Incidence</th><th>Rank in country</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([c, b, i, r]) => (
          <tr><td>{c}</td><td>{b}</td><td class="mono">{i}</td><td class="mono">{r}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    Smaller presences are also recorded in Cuba, Brazil, Peru, Ecuador, Venezuela, England, Ireland,
    Canada, Switzerland, Belgium and the Philippines. Source: Forebears.
  </p>

  <h3>Read the table by density, not by total</h3>
  <p>
    Spain has the largest absolute number, and that is what one would expect of the country of origin.
    But <strong>Uruguay's density is the highest in the world</strong> — 1 in 7,962, against Spain's
    1 in 52,649. A small population carrying a name at six times the homeland's density is the classic
    signature of <strong>founder emigration</strong>: a few people arrived, and their descendants are now a
    measurable fraction of the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    Uruguay and Argentina together make the <strong>River Plate</strong> the true modern heartland of the
    name. The family's own account — Rosario, on the Paraná — sits squarely inside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>South Africa's 61</strong> deserve the same reading. For a surname of 2,124 people worldwide,
    61 in one country far from Iberia and the Americas is not background scatter. It looks like one
    family that arrived and multiplied. <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-spain-one-cluster-and-it-is-not-where-you-would-guess">Spain: one cluster, and it is not where you would guess</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Forebears gives the Spanish distribution as
    <strong>La Rioja 27%</strong>, <strong>Andalusia 25%</strong> and <strong>Madrid 19%</strong> of the
    Spanish total. Read as raw percentages those look like three comparable strongholds. They are nothing
    of the sort. Andalusia and Madrid are simply <em>enormous</em>; La Rioja is tiny.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Share</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Population</th><th>Incidence</th><th>vs. national</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>La Rioja</strong></td><td class="mono">27%</td><td class="mono">~240</td>
          <td class="mono">319,000</td><td class="mono"><strong>1:1,331</strong></td>
          <td class="mono"><strong>39.6&times;</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Andalusia</td><td class="mono">25%</td><td class="mono">~222</td>
          <td class="mono">8,402,000</td><td class="mono">1:37,848</td><td class="mono">1.4&times;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Madrid</td><td class="mono">19%</td><td class="mono">~169</td>
          <td class="mono">6,454,000</td><td class="mono">1:38,255</td><td class="mono">1.4&times;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap">Spain overall</td><td class="mono small">100%</td><td class="mono small">888</td>
          <td class="mono small">46,771,000</td><td class="mono small">1:52,670</td><td class="mono small">1.0&times;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>La Rioja carries the name at forty times the Spanish average.</strong> Andalusia and Madrid
    carry it at 1.4 times — which is to say, at about the rate you would expect if Lerenas were sprinkled
    evenly across Spain and those two regions simply held a lot of people. They are not strongholds. They
    are big.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    There is exactly one real Lerena cluster in Spain, and it is in <strong>La Rioja</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p class="cap">
    Densities calculated by this archive from Forebears' regional percentages and 2014 population figures;
    the arithmetic is in <span class="mono">data/spain-regional.tsv</span>. Forebears leaves the remaining
    29% of the Spanish total unallocated.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-what-the-name-means-the-unresolved-fork">What the name means — the unresolved fork</h2>
  <p>There are two candidate origins, and the archive does not yet know which applies here.</p>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1. Basque: a house-name</h3>
      <p>
        The suffix <strong>-ena</strong> is the Basque possessive, "the house of" or "belonging to" — the
        formative behind <em>Perurena</em>, <em>Marinelarena</em>, <em>Arruabarrena</em> and hundreds more.
        On this reading <em>Lerena</em> is a <strong>house-name</strong>, and in Basque practice the house
        names the family, not the reverse.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        A Basque origin would also fit the destination. Basque emigration to the River Plate began in the
        1820s and ran for a century; it is estimated that up to a tenth of Uruguay's population has at
        least one Basque surname. <span class="chip inf">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2. Castilian: a town in Extremadura</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Llerena</strong>, in Badajoz, is a real and significant town — seat of the Order of
        Santiago and of an Inquisitorial tribunal, with a large medieval Jewish and <em>converso</em>
        population. It has given its name to <strong>36,828</strong> bearers of <em>Llerena</em>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Forebears lists <em>Llerena</em> as the closest variant of <em>Lerena</em> at 92% similarity. The
        Castilian <em>Ll-</em> reduces to <em>L-</em> easily in Spanish-American registers and at ports of
        arrival, so a <em>Lerena</em> may simply be a <em>Llerena</em> written down by someone in a hurry.
        <span class="chip inf">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The Riojan cluster settles it, or very nearly</h3>
  <p>
    The two readings above were written before the Spanish regional figures were in hand. Those figures
    now discriminate between them, and they do not favour the town.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against Llerena in Badajoz:</strong> if <em>Lerena</em> were a worn-down
      <em>Llerena</em>, the name should be thickest in and around <strong>Extremadura</strong>.
      <strong>Extremadura does not appear in the Spanish distribution at all.</strong> The 36,828
      <em>Llerena</em> bearers are a real and separate family of names; the evidence that ours reduced out of
      theirs is now weak.</li>
    <li><strong>For a Basque or upper-Ebro origin:</strong> the one genuine cluster is
      <strong>La Rioja</strong>, on the upper Ebro, immediately south of the Basque provinces and Navarre —
      exactly the country where the Basque possessive <strong>-ena</strong>, "the house of", is at home and
      where Basque house-names spill over into Castilian-speaking territory.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Inferred</span> On the evidence now held, <em>Lerena</em> looks like a
    <strong>Basque-formed house-name of the upper Ebro</strong>, carried from La Rioja or its Basque
    borderlands to the River Plate, and not a reduction of the Extremaduran toponymic.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is <strong>inference from distribution, not proof from a register</strong>, and this archive has
    said throughout that rarity may propose but only documents dispose. It also does not yet touch
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>David's own line</a>, whose earliest certain member was born at
    <strong>Rosario in 1882</strong> and was Argentine by birth. What it does is tell the Spanish end of the
    search where to look first.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:1.6rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; the second test was met, and it
      answered the other way</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>One of the questions below asks whether <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for
        one person in early River Plate records. They do. Following the 1866 Rosario baptism act back
        through the Montevideo family produced a trunk, and at its root a soldier:</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem"><strong>Sancho Llerena Aguilar</strong>, born
        <strong>Córdoba, Andalusia, 1767</strong>, married at <strong>Canelones, Uruguay, 8 January
        1791</strong>, resident at Montevideo, died 1832.</p>
      <p>The Uruguayan parish registers spell him <strong>Lerena</strong> in 1793, 1840 and 1858;
        <strong>Llerena</strong> in 1804, 1811, 1827 and 1830; and once, in 1828,
        <strong><em>Sancho de Llerena</em></strong> &mdash; the toponymic form, <em>of Llerena</em>. A
        Basque house-name in <em>-ena</em> does not take <em>de</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>The distribution argument above still stands on its own terms.</strong> La Rioja really
        is the one dense Spanish cluster of the spelling <em>Lerena</em>, at forty times the national
        average, and that has to be something. What has changed is that it need not be
        <em>this</em> something. <strong>Both can be true:</strong> a Basque-formed <em>Lerena</em> of
        the upper Ebro, and separately a <strong>River Plate <em>Lerena</em> that is a reduction of
        Andalusian <em>Llerena</em></strong>, carried over by one man in the 1780s and smoothed to a
        single <em>l</em> by Uruguayan clerks inside a generation.</p>
      <p>For this archive, only the second bears on anything. The question was never where the name is
        dense in Spain. It was where <em>this family&rsquo;s</em> name came from &mdash; and the only
        Lerena trunk yet found in the River Plate is a <strong>Llerena</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>Tier and caveats, which matter here.</strong> The trunk comes from the
        FamilySearch Family Tree, which is user-submitted and editable by anyone, and the decision that
        those fourteen records are one man is a tree user&rsquo;s, not this archive&rsquo;s &mdash; the
        acts have not been opened. What anyone can check is the narrower fact: the Uruguayan registers
        spell this family both ways. And <strong>nothing connects that trunk to Pablo Armando</strong>;
        no Juan Carlos appears anywhere in it. <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>Hypothesis 1 &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3>What would actually close it</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>Rosario baptism of 22 February 1882</strong>, which names Pablo Armando's parents —
      and, with luck, where they came from. <a href={u("/passport/")}>The passport &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><s>The family's <strong>earliest River Plate records</strong>: which spelling, and do
      <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for one person?</s>
      <strong>Answered 13 September &mdash; they alternate, for Sancho Llerena Aguilar, across forty
      years of Uruguayan registers.</strong> What remains is to open those acts rather than trust the
      attachments.</li>
    <li>An <strong>emigration record</strong> naming the town of origin outright.</li>
    <li>The <strong>La Rioja parish registers</strong> — now a specific place to look rather than a country.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2 id="s-who-the-lerenas-married-at-trinidad">Who the Lerenas married at Trinidad</h2>
<div class="prose">
  <p class="dek">13 September 2026. A small piece of evidence on the Basque-or-Extremaduran fork, from
    a direction the fork itself cannot reach.</p>
  <p>Everything above argues about where the <em>name</em> comes from. This is about the
    <strong>women the men of that name married</strong>, in the one town where the surname is densest
    on earth &mdash; <strong>Trinidad, in the department of Flores</strong>, Uruguay.</p>
  <p>The Trinidad baptisms were re-read by <em>birthplace</em> rather than by surname, which produced
    for the first time what the earlier source gave only as first names: <strong>the mothers&rsquo;
    surnames</strong>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>Wife</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bernab&eacute; Lerena</td><td><strong>Casimira PEL&Aacute;EZ</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vitalino Lerena</td><td><strong>Andrea ECHEVERRY</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cipriano Lerena</td><td><strong>Lauriana BELOQUI</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ysaac Lerena</td><td>Adelaida Gazo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ferm&iacute;n Lerena</td><td>Paulina Barrag&aacute;n</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Florencio Lerena</td><td>Juana Recuero</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Beloqui and Echeverry are Basque</strong> &mdash;
    <em>Beloki</em> and <em>Etxeberri</em> are among the most ordinary surnames in Gipuzkoa and Navarre.
    Pel&aacute;ez is not Basque, but it is the family the Lerenas married <em>repeatedly</em>: by 1885
    the two had to be <strong>dispensed for consanguinity</strong> to marry again, twice in the third
    degree and once in the second and third accumulated. They were one intermarried kindred.</div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What this is worth, stated carefully.</strong> It is evidence
    about the <em>community</em> these Lerenas married into, and that is a different thing from the
    etymology of their name. A family can be Extremaduran by name and marry Basque neighbours; the
    River Plate was full of both. So this <strong>does not settle the fork above</strong>, and it is not
    offered as settling it.</p>
  <p class="cap">What it does do is put a thumb on one side of a scale that has had nothing on it but
    toponymy. Until now the Basque case rested entirely on how the word looks. This is the first
    evidence of the family <em>keeping Basque company</em>.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="s-sources-for-this-page">Sources for this page</h2>
  <p class="cap">
    Forebears (worldwide and per-country figures, accessed 8 September 2026) · Wikipedia, <em>Basque
    surnames</em> and <em>Llerena, Badajoz</em> · NameCensus. The Basque and Castilian readings are
    standard onomastics, not a claim about this family.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-two-spellings-one-town">Two spellings, one town</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Llerena is a town in Badajoz, in Extremadura</strong>, and the surname <em>Llerena</em> is
      toponymic from it — important enough a place that it housed a tribunal of the Inquisition, whose
      eighteenth-century registers surface in any wide search of the name. <em>Lerena</em>, with one L, is
      the commoner form in the Río de la Plata.</p>
    <p>This does not tell us where <em>our</em> family came from. But it explains why the two spellings have
      shadowed one another through every search in this project, and why both have had to be run every
      time — at CEMLA, at findmypast, in the Rosario index, where <strong>L</strong> and <strong>Ll</strong>
      are separate sections and both were read.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-the-forename-is-the-sharper-clue">The forename is the sharper clue</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Searching for the surname has produced null after null. Searching for the <em>given</em> name turns
      out to discriminate better.</p>
    <p><strong>There is no Armando Lerena anywhere in the indexed records of Spain</strong> — not one, in
      any century. Yet <em>Armando</em> is the name this family holds onto across four generations and two
      continents: <strong>Pablo Armando</strong>; his son <strong>Roque Luis Armando</strong>;
      <strong>Anton Armando</strong>, of a 1980 divorce and a 2013 estate; and — found in Uruguay —
      <strong>Gregorio Armando Lerena</strong>, marrying at Flores in 1930.</p>
    <p><strong>Armando is a Río de la Plata name, not a Spanish peninsular one of the 1880s.</strong> It
      came into Spanish through Italian influence, which is the demographic signature of Buenos Aires,
      Rosario and Montevideo in exactly that period — cities remade by Italian immigration — and not of
      rural La Rioja or Guadalajara. Across three entire years of the Rosario baptism index there were
      <strong>two</strong> Armandos. Across four centuries of indexed Spanish records, <strong>none</strong>.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> It is not proof of anything. But of all the tests run in this
      archive, it is the first that argues <em>for</em> one origin over another rather than simply failing
      to find him.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
